WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.280
- What's up, everybody? Michael here.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:08.280
Today, we're serving
up a fresh grilled take

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:11.550
on the hungriest and one of
the horniest sitcom families

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.360
in adult animation, the
Belchers from "Bob's Burgers."

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.270
With 12 seasons and a new
feature film to their name,

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.630
they've cooked up plenty
for us to talk about.

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:21.630
- All that matters is you're a man now

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:24.580
in love with a mannequin
who I'm holding by the arm

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:25.990
but should be in yours.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:27.210
- [Michael] But today, we wanna focus

00:00:27.210 --> 00:00:29.270
on how the Belchers
function as a family unit.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.610
Bob and his wife Linda are
busy running a burger joint,

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.580
and as such, they don't have
a ton of time for their kids,

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:37.120
at least by the standards of
today's helicopter parents.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:39.690
As a result, the children,
Tina, Gene, and Louise,

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.550
get to wander off on their own adventures

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:43.470
while their parents work
to keep the lights on.

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.960
This makes for a well-structured
episode of television,

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.320
but it also begs the question:

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.270
Are Bob and Linda bad parents?

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:51.410
- We don't know all the crazy
crap our kids are doing.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:52.270
- We don't wanna know.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:55.040
- To answer that, we have to
look at how society defines

00:00:55.040 --> 00:00:57.180
good and bad parenting in the first place,

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.410
and we'll also have to ask:

00:00:58.410 --> 00:01:01.770
Are nuclear families even
the best way to raise a kid?

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.930
Let's get into the meat
of it and chomp down

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:06.200
on this "Wisecrack
Edition" on "Bob's Burgers:

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.787
Do You Even Need Your Parents?"

00:01:07.787 --> 00:01:10.440
And of course, spoilers
ahead for the TV show.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.410
Though eccentric, the Belchers
are a fair representation

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.240
of the contemporary American family,

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.750
where the sole male breadwinner

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:18.630
and female homemaker have been replaced

00:01:18.630 --> 00:01:20.670
by two parents working full-time jobs,

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.080
thanks to evolving gender roles

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.637
and changing economic realities.

00:01:23.637 --> 00:01:25.237
- $50? For that?

00:01:25.237 --> 00:01:28.250
- $50. There's a little
girl, a horse, and a zombie.

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:31.000
- [Michael] This leaves parents
with collectively less time

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.310
and energy for their tots.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:34.230
For their part, both Belchers struggle

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:36.380
to find a healthy work-life balance,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:38.710
and without hovering
parentals to interfere,

00:01:38.710 --> 00:01:40.730
the kids are free to frolic about town

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.868
without appointed adult supervision.

00:01:42.868 --> 00:01:43.900
- I don't get it!

00:01:43.900 --> 00:01:44.860
- [Michael] Whether they're exploring

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:47.250
an abandoned taffy
factory or taking a ferry

00:01:47.250 --> 00:01:48.990
to go trick-or-treating
at rich people's houses,

00:01:48.990 --> 00:01:52.660
the Belcher kids are frequently
left to fend for themselves.

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.940
For folks who favor a more
involved mode of childrearing,

00:01:55.940 --> 00:01:57.440
this might set off alarm bells.

00:01:57.440 --> 00:01:59.760
Surely a lack of parental supervision

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:00.980
would leave the Belcher kids

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:04.030
with irreparable feelings
of neglect, right?

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:05.180
Well, not really.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:07.450
In fact, these kids are
pretty well adjusted.

00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.435
Sure, Louise finds herself
in trouble here and there.

00:02:10.435 --> 00:02:11.910
- What are you kids doing?
- What knife?

00:02:11.910 --> 00:02:13.160
- [Michael] And yeah, Tina could probably

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:14.970
use some more help navigating puberty.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.747
- My crotch is itchy.
- Okay.

00:02:16.747 --> 00:02:19.550
Are you telling me as my
daughter or as my grill cook?

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.750
- [Michael] Still, their
issues rarely cause anything

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:22.920
but trivial problems.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:24.330
Critically, though, this is largely

00:02:24.330 --> 00:02:25.680
because of the many neighbors

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:28.290
who pick up the slack when
Bob and Linda aren't around.

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.580
These adults seemingly
share the communal goal

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:33.410
of ensuring that the Belcher
kids turn out all right.

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:36.360
They all step in at various
points to look after the kids

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:38.550
and teach some lessons
that their parents don't.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:39.800
- Gene? Tina?

00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:40.930
What are you two doing here?

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:43.210
- Learning to paint.
- We're honing our craft!

00:02:43.210 --> 00:02:44.783
- Wait, I thought you hated my kids.

00:02:44.783 --> 00:02:47.823
- My artwork shop is open
to all who respect art.

00:02:47.823 --> 00:02:50.000
- [Michael] Because of
their constant interactions

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:52.280
with their adult neighbors,
the Belcher kids are exposed

00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:54.870
to a multitude of different
lifestyles and perspectives.

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:57.410
Take Teddy, the restaurant's
most loyal patron.

00:02:57.410 --> 00:02:59.640
While he's a passive, lonely dude

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:01.920
who's easy for the kids
to target with pranks,

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.420
he's also the first person they call

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:04.253
when they're in trouble.

00:03:04.253 --> 00:03:06.960
Maybe they need to hack into
the school's electrical circuit

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:08.420
for stage magic reasons.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:10.280
Maybe they need someone
to negotiate with the cops

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.510
to keep Tina out of jail,
a mission that helps

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:15.230
to earn him the fleeting
moniker of Uncle Teddy.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.090
While the kids might just be looking out

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.870
for their own self-interest,
Teddy clearly has traits

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:21.970
that they feel their parents lack.

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:23.360
Or take Mr. Frond.

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:25.060
Yes, watching over the Belcher kids

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.650
is technically part of his
job at Wagstaff Elementary.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.230
But as the world's most
intense guidance counselor,

00:03:30.230 --> 00:03:32.060
he takes his students' emotional welfare

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:33.510
extremely seriously.

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:35.060
From him, the kids learn lessons

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:37.330
like perfectionism has its limits-

00:03:37.330 --> 00:03:39.330
- Nobody wants a relatable
guidance counselor!

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:40.910
Kids think I'm perfect.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:42.240
That's why they listen to me.

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:44.150
- [Michael] and don't frame
your students for a crime

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.080
to cover up how you
mishandled school funds,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.470
no matter how delicious
your new coffee pods are.

00:03:48.470 --> 00:03:49.303
- Attention!

00:03:49.303 --> 00:03:52.300
There has been a grisly
crime committed in my office.

00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:54.537
My therapy dolls have been mutilated.

00:03:54.537 --> 00:03:56.010
- [Michael] And because he's so fixated

00:03:56.010 --> 00:03:58.530
on whether or not these kids
are breaking school rules,

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:00.900
the young Belchers are
frequently challenged

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.500
to point out the nuances and gray areas

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.060
of complicated situations,

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.770
even if piping up lands them
in detention or music videos.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:12.587
♪ Let's swap eyes so we can ♪
♪ Empathize ♪

00:04:12.587 --> 00:04:14.890
And don't forget the
extended Belcher family,

00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:16.320
like Linda's sister Gayle.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:17.970
Even though her penchants for crying,

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:20.831
painting animal buttholes, and
sneaking bites of cat food-

00:04:20.831 --> 00:04:23.540
- You gotta warm it up and
put it on your oatmeal.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:25.210
- [Michael] make the kids
a little wary of her,

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:27.380
they know she's ultimately a safe person

00:04:27.380 --> 00:04:28.730
to run to when there's trouble,

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:31.150
like when Louise runs away
to stay with Aunt Gayle

00:04:31.150 --> 00:04:32.650
to avoid seeing the dentist,

00:04:32.650 --> 00:04:35.280
even if she's in for
an intense experience.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.447
- Time to get some shuteye. (screaming)

00:04:38.447 --> 00:04:41.097
The apocalypse is upon us! Why?

00:04:41.097 --> 00:04:45.881
Why must the world end right
when I'm getting sleepy?

00:04:45.881 --> 00:04:46.877
- [Michael] Importantly, Aunt Gayle

00:04:46.877 --> 00:04:49.770
eventually teaches Louise to
be brave and face her fears

00:04:49.770 --> 00:04:52.000
or risk growing up to be like her.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:54.590
- You've seen my life.
- Yeah. It's bad.

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:56.480
- [Michael] But despite
all of her eccentricities,

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.040
the kids understand that
time spent with Aunt Gayle

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.470
is a crash course in radical empathy

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:04.107
and the power of white
lies to keep the peace.

00:05:04.107 --> 00:05:05.750
- I did it, I did it!

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:07.590
I fixed all the paintings.

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:10.750
I added pants and huge, pendulous breasts.

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:11.913
What do you think?

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:14.640
- Gayle, it's beautiful.
- They're great.

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:16.290
- [Michael] Another adult
friend in their community

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:17.960
is Marshmallow, the trans sex worker

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:19.930
whose identity is fully respected.

00:05:19.930 --> 00:05:23.130
Marshmallow acts as a role
model for self-determination,

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.199
independence, and creative expression.

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:27.210
- (gasps) Tall, dark, and handsome.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:29.000
- That settles it! Mom's psychic.

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:29.950
- No, she isn't.

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.771
Marshmallow isn't
handsome. She's beautiful.

00:05:32.771 --> 00:05:33.604
- Blush.

00:05:33.604 --> 00:05:35.870
- [Michael] Even characters who
are supposed to be villains,

00:05:35.870 --> 00:05:37.800
like Mr. Fischoeder, the landlord,

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:41.310
actually accidentally add
value to the kids' lives.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:44.330
Beyond proving that you can
wear white after Labor Day,

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:45.860
Mr. Fischoeder helps the kids

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:47.430
improve their bargaining skills.

00:05:47.430 --> 00:05:49.000
- I take IOUs.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:49.833
- [Michael] At other points,

00:05:49.833 --> 00:05:52.150
his own deeply rotten ethical code

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.270
actually serves as a helpful contrast

00:05:54.270 --> 00:05:56.160
to their own developing moral compasses.

00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:57.280
You know, the basics,

00:05:57.280 --> 00:05:59.330
like "It's bad to threaten
to evict your tenants

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:01.330
unless they pose as your
family at Thanksgiving

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:03.940
just so you can trick a date
into having sex with you."

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:04.773
- Give me those lips.
- No.

00:06:04.773 --> 00:06:05.606
- Lips. Lips.
- No. No.

00:06:05.606 --> 00:06:06.540
- Lips. Lips.
- No. No.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.201
- Lips. Lips.
- Okay, fine.

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:09.488
(Bob screams)
- Whoa.

00:06:09.488 --> 00:06:10.740
- Sick!
- Aw.

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:11.760
- [Michael] Or that you shouldn't

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:13.560
teach children how to cheat at games

00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:15.500
so you can keep your
arcade's profits high.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.260
- It's a good thing I was expecting this

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:18.440
and was able to catch them.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:20.100
- But you taught us to cheat.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:20.933
- Yeah, you did!

00:06:20.933 --> 00:06:22.090
Are you dirty-dogging us?

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.160
- I've never seen you before
in my long and prosperous life.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.280
- [Michael] Although, to
be fair, the kids learn

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.310
some pretty sweet heist
skills from this debacle.

00:06:28.310 --> 00:06:29.530
And just like in real life,

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:31.510
the kids regularly encounter people

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:34.930
whose sense of morality or even
reality is a little tenuous.

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:36.500
- What are those pretty buttons?

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:37.870
- Oh, these patches?

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.980
Well, we got this one for tickling a cop.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.910
- Whoa. What's that one for?

00:06:42.910 --> 00:06:46.250
- For not being associated
with the white power movement.

00:06:46.250 --> 00:06:48.330
- You think you are a mannequin.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:49.290
- Oh, no, it's okay.

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:50.426
You don't need to be scared of me.

00:06:50.426 --> 00:06:51.700
I'm not one of those murder mannequins

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:52.930
or Satanic mannequins.

00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:54.820
- [Michael] Cumulatively,
these interactions with most

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:57.500
of the townsfolk make the
Belcher kids better people.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:58.570
- You're all willing to give up

00:06:58.570 --> 00:07:00.260
your Christmas presents for Chad?

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.130
- [Michael] They're exposed
to more perspectives

00:07:02.130 --> 00:07:03.860
outside of their parents' worldviews,

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:06.310
and they're emboldened and
trusted by their parents

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:08.210
to make their own decisions.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:09.370
Well, for the most part.

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:11.990
- Found a perfectly good mirror
to smash on the way home,

00:07:11.990 --> 00:07:13.860
and everybody made me feel weird about it.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:15.830
- Oh, I love smashing a good mirror.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:16.663
- Lin.
- Right.

00:07:16.663 --> 00:07:18.250
Playing with glass isn't a good idea.

00:07:18.250 --> 00:07:19.940
- [Michael] All three of
the Belcher babies seem

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:21.670
to exude an emotional maturity

00:07:21.670 --> 00:07:23.600
far exceeding that of their peers,

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.100
and you could argue that
it may come directly

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:27.850
from this mode of childrearing.

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:30.340
And Bob and Linda's parenting
style can actually teach us

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:33.010
how to view family in a
radically different way.

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:34.480
- How thoughtful of you all,

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.090
but I don't need a hat to complete me.

00:07:37.090 --> 00:07:39.280
I'm all I need. Just me.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:40.850
- For a long time, it was assumed

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.000
that humans evolved so successfully

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.650
in part due to a family dynamic

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:46.060
wherein males would hunt for food

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:48.420
and portion that food for
their mates and offspring

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:50.700
and the families that did
this the best survived

00:07:50.700 --> 00:07:52.240
to produce more offspring.

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:53.850
But modern anthropologists have started

00:07:53.850 --> 00:07:56.350
to cast doubt on this
hypothesis, suggesting instead

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:59.260
that communal parenting
played a much bigger role

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:00.160
in our evolution.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.070
This method of shared parenting
is called alloparenting.

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.317
As anthropologist David Lancy explains,

00:08:05.317 --> 00:08:07.400
alloparenting is based on "the notion

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:09.470
that all sorts of folks can step up

00:08:09.470 --> 00:08:12.240
and play what we think of as
the normal parenting role.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:14.630
All the various parenting
roles can be distributed,

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:17.690
and collectively those folks
are called alloparents."

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:20.460
This had significant benefits
for prehistoric human tribes.

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:22.370
If a male was bad at hunting

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:24.510
or if a female had lactation issues,

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.870
it didn't necessarily
mean that their offspring

00:08:26.870 --> 00:08:28.800
would have less of a chance of survival.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.240
This sharing of resources
and responsibility led

00:08:31.240 --> 00:08:33.520
to more offspring surviving overall.

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:36.160
Lancy explained, "The
payoff of alloparenting

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:38.780
is that our species grew
very rapidly over time

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:42.050
compared to, say, our nearest
relatives, the chimpanzees."

00:08:42.050 --> 00:08:44.200
Alloparenting is obviously
no longer dominant

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:47.030
in industrialized nations, but
we still implement elements

00:08:47.030 --> 00:08:49.650
of this parenting method
today to an extent.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:52.130
We send our kids to schools
or houses of worship

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:53.080
to learn from experts,

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.560
and parents often share
childcare responsibilities

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:57.490
with their own parents or siblings.

00:08:57.490 --> 00:08:59.470
Those with the resources also might yield

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:01.570
some of their parenting
duties to daycares,

00:09:01.570 --> 00:09:04.550
babysitters, nannies, au
pairs, you get the idea.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:07.890
As the saying goes, it takes
a village to raise a child.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.040
Bob and Linda seem to
instinctively know this,

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:11.760
and they generally trust their own village

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.470
to raise their children in
an organic and communal way

00:09:14.470 --> 00:09:16.160
without the commercial exchange

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:18.060
that comes with babysitting or daycare.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:21.240
They don't try to shield their
kids from pretty much anyone.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:22.970
They have no problem with
their kids hanging out

00:09:22.970 --> 00:09:25.990
with nudists or sex workers
or even bank robbers.

00:09:25.990 --> 00:09:28.880
Bob and Linda don't have to
teach their kids acceptance.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:30.820
It just becomes their default response

00:09:30.820 --> 00:09:32.690
to the different
lifestyles they encounter.

00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:34.720
So what happened to alloparenting?

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.330
Obviously, a lot of social and
cultural changes brought us

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:39.250
from the earliest hunter-gatherer tribes

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:42.330
to modern American life,
but even as late as 1900,

00:09:42.330 --> 00:09:44.700
it was still common to
live in large households

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:46.230
with big extended families.

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.630
This meant more people to offer
childcare, worldly advice,

00:09:49.630 --> 00:09:51.870
shoulders to cry on, et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:53.550
Extended families didn't vanish,

00:09:53.550 --> 00:09:56.010
but rampant industrial
migration into big cities

00:09:56.010 --> 00:09:57.600
bred smaller familial units,

00:09:57.600 --> 00:09:59.860
and this shift was intensely amplified

00:09:59.860 --> 00:10:02.520
by post-World War II cultural ideals of,

00:10:02.520 --> 00:10:04.490
you guessed it, the nuclear family.

00:10:04.490 --> 00:10:07.740
Kids were increasingly siloed
off into units of two parents,

00:10:07.740 --> 00:10:09.910
one breadwinner, one
caretaker, supporting them

00:10:09.910 --> 00:10:11.850
without substantial outside involvement,

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.810
and this was long hailed
in popular culture

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:16.180
as a paragon of healthy child development.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:17.147
Interestingly enough,

00:10:17.147 --> 00:10:19.290
"Bob's Burgers" couples its celebration

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:21.880
of a sort of alloparenting
by frequently critiquing

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:23.970
your typical nuclear family relationships.

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:26.160
For example, Tina's
recurring love interest,

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:29.150
Jimmy Jr., has a far more
dysfunctional relationship

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:30.540
with his father than Tina.

00:10:30.540 --> 00:10:32.440
He resents his father, Jimmy Pesto,

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:35.020
who disapproves of his
son's passion for dance.

00:10:35.020 --> 00:10:37.290
- Don't tell me not to dance, Dad!

00:10:37.290 --> 00:10:39.030
- [Michael] It seems that
Jimmy feels determined

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.720
to pass down his own
conception of masculinity

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:44.350
on to Jimmy Jr., which
doesn't include dancing.

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:48.590
- You see, Bob, I'm a collector
of rare and exotic trophies.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:50.480
- [Michael] And Jimmy Jr.
unfortunately doesn't have

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:52.800
another surrogate father
figure to offer support.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:55.820
Meanwhile, Gene's occasional
love interest, Courtney,

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:58.280
has her life micromanaged
by her own father,

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:00.470
who attempts to live
vicariously through her

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:02.390
to achieve what he never could,

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:04.420
like when he writes a working girl musical

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:05.880
for the kids at school to perform

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:08.500
and tries to make Courtney
pass it off as her own work.

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:09.333
- I wrote a play.

00:11:09.333 --> 00:11:10.630
We're doing it tomorrow. See you there.

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:11.463
- [Michael] Or like when he gets

00:11:11.463 --> 00:11:12.990
a little too emotionally invested

00:11:12.990 --> 00:11:15.680
in being Courtney's temporary
roller-dancing coach.

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:17.520
- You are ruining our dreams!

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:18.570
- I don't even like doing this!

00:11:18.570 --> 00:11:19.609
This is your thing!

00:11:19.609 --> 00:11:20.690
- [Michael] While Bob and Linda are there

00:11:20.690 --> 00:11:22.800
to offer a guiding hand when needed,

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.100
they rarely take a disciplinary approach,

00:11:25.100 --> 00:11:26.260
and they don't really do much

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:28.490
to steer their children's
interest or behavior.

00:11:28.490 --> 00:11:31.250
This allows the kids to fully
explore their identities

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:33.860
while figuring out important
life lessons for themselves.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:35.190
They don't try to suppress

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:38.160
Tina's sometimes robust sexual urges.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:39.630
- [Narrator] She knew what she had to do.

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:42.490
She grabbed Jimmy Jr.'s
butt and changed the world.

00:11:42.490 --> 00:11:44.360
- [Michael] Instead,
they allow her to learn

00:11:44.360 --> 00:11:46.550
and develop healthy boundaries
from those around her,

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:48.640
sometimes by trial and error.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:50.330
- What's Tina doing out there?

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:51.870
- Becoming a woman.
- Oh.

00:11:51.870 --> 00:11:54.490
- I can't tell where your back
ends and your butt begins.

00:11:54.490 --> 00:11:55.890
- [Michael] The Belchers also encourage

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:57.600
Gene's passion for music and theater.

00:11:57.600 --> 00:11:59.410
- [Doug] Stop that singing right now!

00:11:59.410 --> 00:12:01.660
- Nobody tells my son to stop singing!

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:02.600
- [Michael] And while they do their best

00:12:02.600 --> 00:12:05.390
to channel Louise's
anarchistic urges productively,

00:12:05.390 --> 00:12:07.180
they don't use a heavy hand,

00:12:07.180 --> 00:12:09.070
preferring to talk to her like a peer,

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:11.510
like after she lands
in in-school suspension

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:13.540
for pantsing a bully in the cafeteria

00:12:13.540 --> 00:12:15.190
and Linda bails her out.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:16.250
- What you did was wrong.

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:18.620
- Yeah, everybody made that pretty clear.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:20.660
- But I don't want you getting the idea

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:23.240
that helping people isn't
worth it, Louise, 'cause it is.

00:12:23.240 --> 00:12:24.073
- When we consider

00:12:24.073 --> 00:12:27.140
that so many other adults
have significant influence

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:29.210
over the Belcher kids'
lives, we have to ask:

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.100
Are parents all that important

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:32.690
when it comes to raising kids?

00:12:32.690 --> 00:12:34.520
Our collective social fixation

00:12:34.520 --> 00:12:37.940
on the sacred nuclear
family continues today.

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:40.730
Even as our definitions of
marriage and families evolve,

00:12:40.730 --> 00:12:42.200
there's a continued emphasis

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:44.230
on the importance of children being raised

00:12:44.230 --> 00:12:46.640
by two adults in a romantic relationship.

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:50.010
And while there is certainly
data that suggests that divorce

00:12:50.010 --> 00:12:52.600
or absent parents can have
negative impacts on children,

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.270
there's also plenty of
evidence that suggests

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:57.380
that marriage might not be so
great for raising kids either.

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:00.160
Philosopher and children's
rights advocate Samantha Brennan

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:03.060
contends that shifting away
from our cultural hyperfocus

00:13:03.060 --> 00:13:05.510
on marriage can mitigate
"some of the worst dangers

00:13:05.510 --> 00:13:07.020
resulting from the close link

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:08.410
between marriage and parenthood,

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:11.750
which largely result from
the instability, tension,

00:13:11.750 --> 00:13:14.680
and even violence that too
often form a central part

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:17.020
of romantic conflict," which makes sense.

00:13:17.020 --> 00:13:19.670
Witnessing domestic arguments,
or at worst, violence,

00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:21.830
can have devastating effects on children,

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.510
and they can pass that
trauma onto their own kids.

00:13:24.510 --> 00:13:27.190
We see Bob confront this
kind of generational trauma

00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:30.320
when he realizes that in
making his kids stay inside

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:34.460
and work on a fun summer day,
he's being just like his dad.

00:13:34.460 --> 00:13:36.030
- Lin, I just realized something.

00:13:36.030 --> 00:13:38.010
I had a bad childhood.
- Yeah, I know.

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:39.800
- [Michael] Crucially,
he decides then and there

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:42.810
to break that vicious
cycle, at least for the day.

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:44.650
- [Bob] I want you to go
out and have the summer

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:46.550
and the childhood that I never had.

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:48.360
- [Michael] Of course,
this results in the kids

00:13:48.360 --> 00:13:50.034
unwittingly working for drug dealers,

00:13:50.034 --> 00:13:52.210
but at least they learn
some valuable lessons

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:53.274
from that experience.

00:13:53.274 --> 00:13:54.427
- And if the po-po rolls up,

00:13:54.427 --> 00:13:56.130
the first call is to your
lawyer, not your mom.

00:13:56.130 --> 00:13:58.130
- It seems that every
generation of parents

00:13:58.130 --> 00:14:00.170
faces a new moral panic

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:02.240
about what their children
are being exposed to.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.490
Whether it's rock and roll, video games,

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:06.760
or "Dungeons &amp; Dragons,"
there's no shortage

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:09.680
of external forces to blame
for corrupting the youth.

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.270
It's natural for parents to want

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:13.890
to shield their children
from harm, but sometimes,

00:14:13.890 --> 00:14:16.870
that sheltering can be
harmful in and of itself.

00:14:16.870 --> 00:14:19.470
One American Psychological
Association study found

00:14:19.470 --> 00:14:22.060
that "children with helicopter
parents may be less able

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:24.600
to deal with the challenging
demands of growing up,

00:14:24.600 --> 00:14:27.430
especially with navigating the
complex school environment"

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:29.910
and had more trouble
regulating their own emotions

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:30.890
and making friends.

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:33.350
Giving kids autonomy, then, is important.

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.700
What's more, by giving
their children free rein,

00:14:35.700 --> 00:14:37.470
the Belchers effectively outsource

00:14:37.470 --> 00:14:39.940
some of their parental duties
to their family, friends,

00:14:39.940 --> 00:14:42.720
and neighbors, lessening their
own strain in the process.

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:45.170
Nowadays, despite its
continued veneration,

00:14:45.170 --> 00:14:48.130
the reality is that many
families no longer look nuclear,

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:50.940
often being composed of
single or divorced parents.

00:14:50.940 --> 00:14:52.930
In this landscape, extended family

00:14:52.930 --> 00:14:56.060
or surrogate parent figures
become more important than ever,

00:14:56.060 --> 00:14:58.410
particularly if your family isn't loaded.

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:00.960
While wealthy families of
any formation can afford

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:02.830
to pay for alloparenting privileges

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:04.920
like daycares or bassoon lessons,

00:15:04.920 --> 00:15:07.610
the biggest consequences of
collapsed social networks

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:10.580
and extended families hit
those with the least resources.

00:15:10.580 --> 00:15:13.330
Even conservative columnist
David Brooks argues

00:15:13.330 --> 00:15:16.210
that successfully maintaining
a nuclear family today

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:19.617
has a lot more to do with class
than anything else, writing,

00:15:19.617 --> 00:15:22.380
"Affluent conservatives often
pat themselves on the back

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:24.090
for having stable nuclear families.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:25.290
They preach that everybody else

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:26.740
should build stable families too.

00:15:26.740 --> 00:15:29.300
But then they ignore
one of the main reasons

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:30.930
their own families are stable:

00:15:30.930 --> 00:15:32.490
They can afford to purchase the support

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:34.450
that extended family used to provide

00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:35.790
and that the people they preach at,

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:37.820
further down the income scale, cannot."

00:15:37.820 --> 00:15:39.030
It's like a real-life version

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:40.770
of that famous ClickHole headline.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:42.890
At the same time, we're seeing less social

00:15:42.890 --> 00:15:45.840
and financial support being
provided to families than ever,

00:15:45.840 --> 00:15:47.950
with the slashing of the
Child Tax Credit sending

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:51.270
almost 4 million kids into
poverty seemingly overnight.

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.370
Nuclear and non-nuclear families alike

00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:55.180
who lack economic resources are faced

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:57.510
with unreasonable burdens and expectations

00:15:57.510 --> 00:15:59.290
of what good parenting should look like.

00:15:59.290 --> 00:16:00.660
For the working-class Belchers,

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:02.560
their solution, to rely
on friends and family,

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:04.310
might just be the best way to cope

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:07.100
with the realities that
many families face today.

00:16:07.100 --> 00:16:09.190
And as we see with the
Belchers, this support

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:12.090
can ultimately make the
family unit even stronger.

00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:15.170
The comfort of knowing everyone
is safe and well cared for,

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:17.670
well, most of the time, alleviates stress.

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:21.060
Each Belcher is free to grow
as a person on their own terms.

00:16:21.060 --> 00:16:23.170
In other words, the Belchers
prove that you don't have

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:25.480
to spend all your time
with your nuclear family

00:16:25.480 --> 00:16:27.020
to still love the (beep) out of 'em.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:29.440
- Kids, I love you, and
Linda, I love you too,

00:16:29.440 --> 00:16:31.292
almost as much as the kids, but not....

00:16:31.292 --> 00:16:33.500
- Oh, Bobby, I love you too.

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:35.090
- [Michael] What's more,
"Bob's Burgers" shows us

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:37.260
that a society structured
to allow children

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:39.120
to reap the benefits of alloparenting

00:16:39.120 --> 00:16:40.570
rather than making raising children

00:16:40.570 --> 00:16:42.810
exclusively the domain
of the nuclear family

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:44.590
is one that fosters the development

00:16:44.590 --> 00:16:48.150
of smart, savvy, and yes,
occasionally devious tots.

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:49.220
But what do you guys think?

00:16:49.220 --> 00:16:52.330
Are the Belchers a model for
a more adaptive family unit?

00:16:52.330 --> 00:16:53.970
Do nuclear families ruin us?

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:55.010
Let us know in the comments.

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:56.960
Thanks to our patrons
for all your support,

00:16:56.960 --> 00:17:00.300
and don't forget to check
out our new perks on Patreon

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:02.840
and check out our new
stream, "Wisecrack Live,"

00:17:02.840 --> 00:17:05.030
Thursdays at 11 a.m. Pacific time.

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:06.110
Hit that subscribe button

00:17:06.110 --> 00:17:08.180
like you're Thomas Edison
electrocuting Topsy,

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:10.150
and don't forget to ring that bell.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:12.560
As always, thanks for
watching, and remember,

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:14.697
I love you, but you're all terrible.

00:17:14.697 --> 00:17:17.280
(upbeat music)

